+<h2 class="h2">Who this serves</h2><div class="rule"></div><p>Fort Lauderdale bookings split three ways: airport transfers through FLL, cruise transfers to Port Everglades, and corporate or hotel travel around Las Olas Boulevard and downtown. The airport and the port are near each other but have separate entrances and traffic patterns, and some trips involve both — a flight arrival followed by a cruise departure, or the reverse — on different schedules with their own timing considerations.</p><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:40px">The geography</h2><div class="rule"></div><p>I-95 runs the length of Broward County along the eastern side of the city, connecting Fort Lauderdale south to Miami-Dade and north toward Palm Beach County. I-595 branches east-west, feeding directly into FLL and Port Everglades from I-95 and Florida's Turnpike. Las Olas Boulevard, the city's downtown corridor, sits just north of the airport and port complex, closer to the beach than most visitors expect.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="prose reveal">
+<h2 class="h2">Airport access</h2><div class="rule"></div><p>Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International (FLL) is the local airport and the default choice for most trips originating here. Miami International (MIA) is a reasonable alternative to the south for specific routes or fares. President Donald J. Trump International Airport (DJT, formerly PBI) to the north is closer from Fort Lauderdale than from most of Miami-Dade, and worth considering for flights out of West Palm Beach.</p><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:40px">Trip types</h2><div class="rule"></div><p>Cruise transfers to Port Everglades use flight tracking when the passenger is also arriving by air that day. Cruise check-in and departure times are fixed by the cruise line, not adjustable around a delayed flight, so arriving in Fort Lauderdale the day before a sailing is generally the lower-risk plan; a same-day flight-to-ship connection can work but carries the flight's own delay risk. Corporate travel around Las Olas and downtown Fort Lauderdale runs on the same accounts used for Miami-based firms with a Broward office or client.</p><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:40px">A note on I-95 Express Lanes</h2><div class="rule"></div><p>The stretch of I-95 serving Fort Lauderdale includes tolled Express Lanes alongside the free general lanes. They can meaningfully change trip timing at peak hours, and using them is a routing decision made before dispatch, not an add-on discovered mid-trip.</p>
