 <div class="rule"></div>
-<p>Executives based in the Sunny Isles towers get the same accountable-account service as Brickell — recurring travel, hourly and as-directed days, one point of contact from quote to drop-off.</p>
-<h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:40px">Dining &amp; events</h2>
+<ul class="points">
+<li><i class="mark"></i><span><b>Two bridges, no alternative.</b> The 163rd Street Bridge and the William Lehman Causeway (192nd Street) are the only vehicle routes to the mainland.</span></li><li><i class="mark"></i><span><b>163rd Street Bridge openings.</b> This one is a drawbridge and can open for boat traffic on a set schedule, holding vehicles on both sides until it closes. The William Lehman Causeway crosses on a fixed span and isn't subject to openings.</span></li><li><i class="mark"></i><span><b>Collins Avenue.</b> The single road running the length of the island — every address here sits on or just off it, and it is the main source of local congestion at peak times.</span></li><li><i class="mark"></i><span><b>South to Bal Harbour, north to Hallandale.</b> Collins Avenue continues south into Bal Harbour Shops and north toward Hollywood Beach and Broward County.</span></li><li><i class="mark"></i><span><b>Peak-season congestion.</b> Collins Avenue slows noticeably during winter season and holiday weekends, when both bridges see heavier inbound and outbound traffic at similar times.</span></li>
+</ul>
